implementationPlan.description:
RED first (load test-driven-development).

LIVE-LAUNCHER SAFETY (read first). Every live seat hot-reloads scripts/moe-agent.sh (exec) and scripts/moe-agent.ps1 (in-process supervisor) as soon as the file changes, then re-runs this exact pre-flight. Untested or half-done launcher bytes must never sit in the checkout, so work in a MIRROR until step 4:
- M="$(wslpath "$(cmd.exe /d /c 'echo %TEMP%' 2>/dev/null | tr -d '\r')")/moe-ad0d-$$"; mkdir -p "$M/scripts/tests" "$M/pristine". The mirror sits under the Windows temp so both WSL node and Windows node can run it.
- Copy scripts/moe-agent.sh and scripts/moe-agent.ps1 into both $M/scripts/ and $M/pristine/.
- The new test reads ../moe-agent.{sh,ps1} relative to its own file, so $M/scripts/tests/daemon-json-cap.test.mjs exercises the mirror.

Prerequisites task-bdf6db6f, task-bc8410af and task-c2a900aa (and, through bdf6db6f, task-6aa460bb) edit both launchers. Take the mirror only after they have landed, and locate the blocks by anchor, never by line number.

Write $M/scripts/tests/daemon-json-cap.test.mjs (node:test, node builtins only). Model it on scripts/tests/seat-registration.test.mjs:
- read(ext) with .replaceAll('\r\n', '\n');
- between(source, start, end), which asserts both anchors;
- fwd/shq/psq quoting;
- PYTHON = windows ? 'python' : 'python3';
- its ENGINES matrix with probe(): bash ('Git Bash' at C:/Program Files/Git/bin/bash.exe on Windows); PowerShell 5.1, skipped with a reason off Windows or when absent; PowerShell 7 (pwsh), skipped with a reason when absent. A skipped engine is reported as skipped, never counted as a pass.

SEGMENTS, sliced inside each test so that a missing anchor fails only that test. Anchor each boundary WITH its leading newline and exact indentation: the same text also appears deeper-indented (the FOREIGN branch has its own `if [ "$RUNNING" = false ]; then`, which a bare indexOf would hit first).
- sh helper: between(sh, '\ndaemon_json_field() {', '\n# Start daemon if needed'). Slice it only if present, else use ''. That way today's launchers fail on behavior rather than on a missing anchor; case (e) separately requires the helper.
- sh check: between(sh, '\n    if [ -f "$DAEMON_INFO" ]; then', '\n    if [ "$RUNNING" = false ]; then').
- ps1 helper: between(ps, '\nfunction Read-MoeDaemonJsonText(', '\nif (-not $NoStartDaemon) {'), likewise optional.
- ps1 check: between(ps, '\n    if (Test-Path $daemonInfoPath) {', '\n    if (-not $running) {').

FIXTURES. Each lives in its own mkdtemp dir, removed in finally. daemon.json = JSON.stringify(info) padded with trailing spaces to an exact byte length (assert Buffer.byteLength).
- sh info = { port: 9876, startedAt: 'x', projectPath: '/moe-cap/fixture' }, with no pid: Git Bash's kill -0 cannot see a Windows PID.
- ps1 info adds pid: process.pid; Get-Process finds the test runner on every OS.
- Malformed = the bytes '{not json'.
Never use a fixture larger than 65537 bytes, and never /dev/zero (mem:gotcha-dev-zero-red-run-runaway).

sh SCRIPT, run with --noprofile --norc:
- 'set -e' (the launcher runs under it);
- RED='' GREEN='' YELLOW='' NC='';
- PYTHON_CMD=<PYTHON>, DAEMON_INFO=<shq(fwd(file))>, RUNNING=false, FOREIGN_DAEMON=false;
- then the helper and the check;
- then echo "@@ port=[$DAEMON_PORT] project=[$DAEMON_PROJECT] running=$RUNNING".

ps1 SCRIPT, run with -NoProfile -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File:
- $ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop' (the launcher's);
- $daemonInfoPath = <psq(file)>, $running = $false;
- then the helper and the check;
- then Write-Output "@@ running=$running port=$($info.port) err=$(if ($Error.Count) { $Error[0].Exception.Message })".
Use spawnSync with a 60 s timeout.

CASES, per engine:
(a) 65536 bytes: exit 0.
   - sh prints no 'Failed to parse daemon.json', and prints '@@ port=[9876] project=[/moe-cap/fixture] running=false'.
   - ps1 prints '@@ running=True port=9876'.
(b) 65537 bytes of valid JSON: exit 0, so the script reached its last line.
   - sh prints '[WARN] Failed to parse daemon.json: daemon.json is larger than 64 KiB', 'Will attempt to start a new daemon' and '@@ port=[] project=[] running=false'.
   - ps1 prints '@@ running=False port= err=daemon.json is larger than 64 KiB'.
   - The fixture still exists at 65537 bytes.
(c) Malformed: exit 0.
   - sh prints '[WARN] Failed to parse daemon.json:' and '@@ port=[] project=[] running=false'.
   - ps1 prints '@@ running=False port=', with no 'larger than' in err.
(d) PowerShell engines only: the whole moe-agent.ps1 parses. [System.Management.Automation.Language.Parser]::ParseFile(<absolute path>, [ref]$null, [ref]$errors) must report zero errors; print them on failure.
(e) Static, engine-independent:
   - no line of moe-agent.sh contains both '$PYTHON_CMD -c' and 'DAEMON_INFO';
   - no line of moe-agent.ps1 contains both 'Get-Content' and '$daemonInfoPath';
   - the sh helper segment contains 'read(65537)' and '>65536';
   - the ps1 helper segment contains 'byte[] 65537' and '-gt 65536';
   - both files contain 'daemon.json is larger than 64 KiB' (string parity).

RED, with WSL node, on the mirror: node --test "$M/scripts/tests/daemon-json-cap.test.mjs". On today's launchers:
- bash (b) fails, because the 65537 file parses;
- bash (c) fails, because set -e aborts at the unguarded PID_OUTPUT=$(...) before its WARN. The architect verified the shell exits 1 there;
- (e) fails;
- (a) passes;
- the PowerShell cases are skipped on WSL.
Record that in the step note.

GREEN for ps1, in the MIRROR ($M/scripts/moe-agent.ps1). Step 4 replays it into scripts/moe-agent.ps1.

Keep the UTF-8 BOM (bytes EF BB BF) and the LF line endings. A tool that drops the BOM makes Windows PowerShell 5.1 read the file as ANSI and fail to parse it; parity-check.sh checks for this.

1. Add, immediately before 'if (-not $NoStartDaemon) {', in the same idiom as the existing Read-MoeGateTail:

# daemon.json is repository content: read at most 64 KiB + 1 byte so a huge or
# never-ending file cannot stall pre-flight; the callers' catch treats the throw
# like unparseable JSON. Same cap as moe-agent.sh's daemon_json_field.
function Read-MoeDaemonJsonText([string]$Path) {
    $stream = [IO.File]::OpenRead($Path)
    try {
        $buffer = New-Object byte[] 65537
        $total = 0
        while ($total -lt $buffer.Length) {
            $read = $stream.Read($buffer, $total, $buffer.Length - $total)
            if ($read -le 0) { break }
            $total += $read
        }
        if ($total -gt 65536) { throw 'daemon.json is larger than 64 KiB' }
        return [Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetString($buffer, 0, $total).TrimStart([char]0xFEFF)
    } finally { $stream.Dispose() }
}

Notes on the helper:
- $daemonInfoPath is absolute ($projectPath comes from Resolve-Path), so .NET's separate current directory does not matter.
- TrimStart keeps Get-Content's BOM tolerance.

2. Replace both reads, each inside its existing try:
- $info = Read-MoeDaemonJsonText $daemonInfoPath | ConvertFrom-Json
- in the wait loop: $newInfo = Read-MoeDaemonJsonText $daemonInfoPath | ConvertFrom-Json
Error handling is each site's existing catch: $running = $false, or keep waiting.

Then run the mirror on Windows as well as WSL. From WSL: (cd "$M" && cmd.exe /d /c "node --test scripts\tests\daemon-json-cap.test.mjs"). Windows node 24, Git Bash, PowerShell 5.1 and pwsh 7 all exist on this box, so every case, including (d), runs there and none may be skipped. If the seat runs where no Windows host is reachable, report_blocked: PowerShell coverage is not optional for a ps1 change.

Both legs must be green.

Adversarial self-review (load adversarial-self-review) of the three-file diff. Check:
- the off-by-one at 65536/65537 in both shells;
- the ps1 stream is disposed on every path, including the throw;
- 'py -3' still word-splits;
- only the PID read needed the set -e guard, and the other three reads keep their '|| echo ""';
- an oversize file is never deleted (only the stale-PID branch deletes, after a successful parse);
- the WARN prints python's one-line message, never file content;
- the test removes its temp dirs and spawns with timeouts;
- every anchor is newline-anchored;
- no other daemon.json read is left: moe-call.sh only checks existence, and moe-team never reads it.

State in the completion summary, as an INTENDED behavior change: on sh, a malformed daemon.json now WARNs and starts a daemon, matching ps1, instead of silently aborting the launcher under set -e.

Residuals, pre-existing, noted only:
- a daemon is started while one is live but its daemon.json is unreadable, exactly as ps1 does today;
- a FIFO at .moe/daemon.json blocks the open, but git cannot commit FIFOs.

If the review changes bytes, make the change in the mirror and repeat step 4 items 1-5. Report the three paths in complete_step.modifiedFiles.

completionSummary:
Both launchers now read .moe/daemon.json through one bounded read of at most 65537 bytes. A larger file takes the existing unparseable path.
- moe-agent.sh: new daemon_json_field helper. It does python open(p,'rb').read(65537), exits with 'daemon.json is larger than 64 KiB' above 65536, and writes via sys.stdout.write. All 4 reads use it (PID, port, projectPath, wait-loop PID). The PID read gains `|| PID_EXIT_CODE=$?`.
- moe-agent.ps1: new Read-MoeDaemonJsonText, a FileStream into byte[65537] with the same throw, disposed in finally. Both Get-Content reads use it. BOM kept; parity passes.

INTENDED behavior change (sh): a malformed or vanished daemon.json now prints '[WARN] Failed to parse daemon.json' and starts a daemon, as ps1 does. Before, the unguarded assignment under set -e silently aborted the launcher, so that WARN was dead code.

Deviation from the plan, verified: the ps1 helper opens `Convert-Path -LiteralPath $Path`. Resolve-Path makes a UNC project's path provider-qualified (`Microsoft.PowerShell.Core\FileSystem::\\...`). [IO.File]::OpenRead rejects that form; Get-Content accepted it. A ps1 test case covers it.

New scripts/tests/daemon-json-cap.test.mjs (14 tests) runs the real check blocks on bash/Git Bash, PS 5.1 and pwsh 7:
- 65536-byte ok;
- 65537-byte takes the WARN/catch path and the file is kept;
- malformed;
- the UNC path form;
- ParseFile;
- static checks: no unbounded read is left, the cap literals, the shared message.

Results:
- RED on pristine launchers: WSL bash (b)/(c) and the static check fail; on Windows Git Bash (b)/(c) and PS (b) fail too.
- GREEN: WSL 4 pass / 10 PS skipped; Windows 14/14, 0 skipped.
- Mutants (no Convert-Path, -gt 65537) are caught on both PS engines.
- Edits went through a %TEMP% mirror, then a compare-and-swap Edit replay; the checkout is cmp-identical to the mirror.

Residuals (pre-existing):
- a live daemon with an unreadable daemon.json gets a second start, as on ps1;
- a FIFO blocks the open;
- the malformed
